('112.45.c.7.B',3,'What is the gravitational potential energy of a 2 kg object at a height of 10 m? (g ≈ 9.8 m/s²)','["19.6 J", "98 J", "196 J", "20 J"]'::jsonb,2,'PE = mgh.','original'),
('112.45.c.7.B',4,'If a car''s speed doubles, its kinetic energy is multiplied by what factor?','["16", "2", "8", "4"]'::jsonb,3,'Kinetic energy is proportional to speed squared.','original'),
('112.45.c.7.B',3,'Mechanical energy of a system is the sum of what?','["kinetic and potential energy", "mass and speed", "work and power", "force and distance"]'::jsonb,0,'ME = KE + PE.','original'),
('112.45.c.7.C',3,'A ball falls from a shelf. What happens to its energy as it falls?','["kinetic energy changes into potential energy", "gravitational potential energy changes into kinetic energy", "energy is destroyed", "its energy stays potential"]'::jsonb,1,'Total mechanical energy stays about the same without friction.','original'),
('112.45.c.7.C',3,'On a roller coaster without friction, where is the kinetic energy greatest?','["it is the same everywhere", "at the start of the climb only", "at the lowest point", "at the highest point"]'::jsonb,2,'The speed is greatest at the bottom.','original'),
('112.45.c.7.C',4,'The work-energy theorem states that the net work done on an object equals what?','["its change in mass", "its final position", "its change in volume", "its change in kinetic energy"]'::jsonb,3,'W = ΔKE.','original'),
('112.45.c.7.C',3,'When a sliding box stops because of friction, where does its kinetic energy go?','["into thermal energy", "it disappears", "into magnetic energy", "into gravitational potential energy"]'::jsonb,0,'Friction transforms motion energy into heat.','original'),
('112.45.c.7.C',4,'A ball dropped from 5 m has about what speed just before it hits the ground? (g ≈ 9.8 m/s², ignore air)','["49 m/s", "9.9 m/s", "98 m/s", "5 m/s"]'::jsonb,1,'v = √(2gh).','original'),
('112.45.c.7.D',3,'What is the momentum of a 1000 kg car moving at 20 m/s?','["1020 kg·m/s", "980 kg·m/s", "20,000 kg·m/s", "50 kg·m/s"]'::jsonb,2,'p = mv.','original'),
('112.45.c.7.D',3,'Impulse is equal to what?','["the change in mass", "the change in energy", "the total work", "the change in momentum"]'::jsonb,3,'J = FΔt = Δp.','original'),
('112.45.c.7.D',3,'How does an airbag reduce injury in a collision?','["it increases the time over which the person stops, reducing the force", "it increases the force on the person", "it decreases the person''s mass", "it makes the car go faster"]'::jsonb,0,'The same change in momentum over a longer time means less force.','original'),
('112.45.c.7.D',4,'Why do crumple zones on cars help protect passengers?','["they increase momentum", "they lengthen the stopping time, reducing the average force", "they stop the car instantly", "they make the car heavier"]'::jsonb,1,'Impulse is the same but force is smaller.','original'),
('112.45.c.7.E',3,'In an inelastic collision, what happens to the total kinetic energy?','["it disappears from the universe", "it is conserved exactly", "some is converted to other forms such as heat", "it doubles"]'::jsonb,2,'Momentum is conserved but kinetic energy is not.','original'),
('112.45.c.7.E',3,'In a perfectly elastic collision, what is conserved?','["only kinetic energy", "neither", "only mass", "both momentum and kinetic energy"]'::jsonb,3,'No kinetic energy is lost.','original'),
('112.45.c.7.E',4,'A 2 kg cart moving at 3 m/s hits a 1 kg cart at rest, and they stick together. What is their speed afterward?','["2 m/s", "6 m/s", "3 m/s", "1 m/s"]'::jsonb,0,'6 kg·m/s ÷ 3 kg.','original'),
('112.45.c.7.E',3,'A gun fires a bullet forward and recoils backward. Which principle explains the recoil?','["conservation of charge", "conservation of momentum", "Snell''s law", "Ohm''s law"]'::jsonb,1,'Total momentum starts at zero and stays at zero.','original'),
('112.45.c.8.A',3,'On what does the period of a simple pendulum mainly depend (for small angles)?','["its mass", "how far it started from the middle only", "its length", "the color of the bob"]'::jsonb,2,'Longer pendulums swing more slowly.','original'),
('112.45.c.8.A',3,'A wave has a period of 0.5 s. What is its frequency?','["5 Hz", "0.5 Hz", "0.25 Hz", "2 Hz"]'::jsonb,3,'f = 1 ÷ T.','original'),
('112.45.c.8.A',3,'A mass on a spring moves fastest at what point?','["the equilibrium position", "it moves at constant speed", "the maximum displacement", "the top of the motion only"]'::jsonb,0,'All of the energy is kinetic at equilibrium.','original'),
('112.45.c.8.A',4,'What kind of force acts on an object in simple harmonic motion?','["no force", "a restoring force toward the equilibrium position", "a force away from the center", "a constant force in one direction"]'::jsonb,1,'The force is proportional to displacement.','original'),
('112.45.c.8.A',3,'What does a wave transfer from one place to another?','["charge only", "mass", "energy", "matter"]'::jsonb,2,'The medium vibrates but does not travel with the wave.','original'),
('112.45.c.8.B',2,'Which of these waves is longitudinal?','["light", "radio waves", "X-rays", "sound"]'::jsonb,3,'Sound consists of compressions and rarefactions.','original'),
('112.45.c.8.B',3,'In a transverse wave, how does the medium vibrate?','["perpendicular to the direction the wave travels", "it does not vibrate", "parallel to the direction the wave travels", "in a circle"]'::jsonb,0,'Like a wave on a rope.','original'),
('112.45.c.8.B',3,'Which type of wave can travel through a vacuum?','["sound waves", "electromagnetic waves", "waves on a rope", "water waves"]'::jsonb,1,'They do not need a medium.','original'),
('112.45.c.8.B',3,'What are the regions of high pressure in a sound wave called?','["rarefactions", "crests", "compressions", "nodes"]'::jsonb,2,'Particles are pushed close together.','original'),
('112.45.c.8.C',3,'A wave has a frequency of 5 Hz and a wavelength of 2 m. What is its speed?','["2.5 m/s", "7 m/s", "0.4 m/s", "10 m/s"]'::jsonb,3,'v = fλ.','original'),
('112.45.c.8.C',3,'What property of a sound wave determines how loud it is?','["amplitude", "speed only", "period", "wavelength"]'::jsonb,0,'Greater amplitude carries more energy.','original'),
('112.45.c.8.C',3,'What property of a sound wave determines its pitch?','["mass of the medium", "frequency", "amplitude", "color"]'::jsonb,1,'Higher frequency sounds higher.','original'),
('112.45.c.8.C',4,'A wave''s speed stays the same while its frequency doubles. What happens to the wavelength?','["it quadruples", "it stays the same", "it is halved", "it doubles"]'::jsonb,2,'v = fλ.','original'),
('112.45.c.8.D',2,'What is it called when a wave bounces off a surface?','["diffraction", "absorption", "refraction", "reflection"]'::jsonb,3,'The law of reflection applies.','original'),
('112.45.c.8.D',3,'What causes refraction?','["a change in wave speed when entering a different medium", "a change in frequency", "the wave stopping", "amplitude doubling"]'::jsonb,0,'The wave bends as its speed changes.','original'),
('112.45.c.8.D',3,'What is diffraction?','["the making of waves", "the bending of waves around obstacles or through openings", "the stopping of waves", "the bouncing of waves"]'::jsonb,1,'It is strongest when the opening is similar in size to the wavelength.','original'),
('112.45.c.8.D',3,'When two waves overlap and make a larger amplitude, this is called what?','["refraction", "reflection", "constructive interference", "destructive interference"]'::jsonb,2,'Crests align with crests.','original')
) as v(code, diff, prompt, choices, ans, expl, src)
